notes.body:
I'm hoping this will make happy-shark more usable so that we can start moving dissector tests there.

You can test this by doing the following:

* Check out a copy of the [happy-shark](https://github.com/wireshark/happy-shark) repository.
* Place the following in `happy-shark/wireshark-tests.json`:
```
{
  "case_name": "happy_shark",
  "tests": [
    {
      "test_name": "dns",
      "tshark_args": [ "-r", "${case_dir}/tests/dns-1/dns.pcapng",
		  "-Y", "dns", "-T", "fields", "-e", "dns.qry.name"
		  ],
      "required_output": [ "in.m.yahoo.com" ]
    }
  ]
}
```
* Run `../test/test.py -p ./run --add-external-tests /path/to/happy-shark/wireshark-tests.json suite_external.case_happy_shark.test_dns` from your Wireshark build directory.

`wireshark-tests.json` should now look like this:

```
{
"case_name": "happy_shark",
"tests": [
  {
    "test_name": "dns",
    "tshark_args": [ "-r", "${case_dir}/tests/dns-1/dns.pcapng",
        "-Y", "dns", "-T", "fields", "-e", "dns.qry.name"
        ],
    "requirements": [
        [ "count", "in.m.yahoo.com", 1 ],
        [ "grep", "in.m.yahoo.com" ],
        [ "!grep", "in.m.notyahoo.com" ],
        [ "in", "in.m.yahoo.com", 0 ],
        [ "!in", "in.m.notyahoo.com", 0 ]
    ]
  }
]
}
```

This should cover many (most?) of our dissection tests, which means we could move many of them to happy_shark.
